    pr_ref:  # 27b5
    description: "Add a way for `scripts/tracker` to enumerate every tag currently in use across the tracker corpus, so the existing-coverage check at structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md §\"When NOT to file a new tracker item\" point 1 can be performed reliably without guessing tag names.\n\n**Motivating gap (jgstern message on WI-mobup, 2026-04-27T14:17:40Z):** the playbook's spot-check step tells the agent to run `scripts/tracker list --tag <plausible-tag>`. That step assumes the agent already knows which tags exist — but `scripts/tracker` has no enumeration command. `--tag` only filters by an *already-known* value; `--help` lists no `tags` subcommand; `batch` is unrelated. The result is the agent guesses tags from memory, which produces low-quality coverage checks (the very failure mode WI-mobup tried to prevent).\n\n**Proposed CLI shapes (pick one in the implementation PR):**\n\n1. New top-level subcommand `scripts/tracker tags`:\n     - `tracker tags`            → newline-separated list of all tags, sorted\n     - `tracker tags --count`    → \"tag\\tcount\" rows, sorted by count desc then alpha\n     - `tracker tags --json`     → `{ \"<tag>\": <count>, … }`\n2. New flag on `list`: `scripts/tracker list --list-tags` — same output as #1.\n\nEither shape works. The subcommand form is more discoverable in `--help`; the flag form is cheaper to wire (one place to edit). Lean toward subcommand for discoverability.\n\n**Scope:**\n- Read-only enumeration over the compiled tier index (canonical ∪ workspace by default; add `--tier stealth` later if needed).\n- Should be cheap: tags are already a field on every compiled item, so this is one pass through the in-memory model.\n- Output stable / sorted so it's pipeable into `xargs` / `comm` without surprises.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Empty corpus → empty output, exit 0.\n- Corpus with 3 items having tags `[a, b]` / `[b, c]` / `[a]` → `a\\nb\\nc\\n` (sorted).\n- `--count` form returns counts matching expected occurrences.\n- Tier filter respected (stealth-only items excluded by default).\n\n**Cross-references:**\n- WI-mobup-matih-folit-maraj-zigid-zopab-bibat-vokak — re-opened pending this; isbefore edge points there.\n- `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md` §\"When NOT to file a new tracker item\" point 1 (the call-site that this enables).\n- `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/cli.py` — where the new subcommand or flag lands."  # 27b5
    fields: {}  # 27b5
- op: update  # 879a
  at: '2026-04-27T14:33:00Z'  # 879a
  by: agent  # 879a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 879a
  clock: 2  # 879a
  nonce: 879a  # 879a
  set:  # 879a
    description: "Add a minimal but useful tag management/organization layer to `scripts/tracker`. The trigger is enumeration (so the existing-coverage check at structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md §\"When NOT to file a new tracker item\" point 1 can actually run), but the same mechanic exposes the rename / merge / describe operations that keep the tag namespace coherent over time.\n\n**Motivating gap (jgstern message on WI-mobup, 2026-04-27T14:17:40Z + follow-up 14:33Z):** the playbook's spot-check step tells the agent to run `scripts/tracker list --tag <plausible-tag>`. That step assumes the agent already knows which tags exist — but `scripts/tracker` has no enumeration command. `--tag` only filters by an *already-known* value; `--help` lists no `tags` subcommand. The result is the agent guesses tags from memory, which produces low-quality coverage checks (the very failure mode WI-mobup tried to prevent). Layered on top: with no rename / merge / describe story, near-duplicates accumulate (`developer_experience` vs `dx`, `bakeoff_infrastructure` vs `bakeoff_infra`) and silently dilute filterability over time.\n\n**Proposed CLI shape — new top-level `tracker tags` subcommand:**\n\n| Form | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `tracker tags`                       | List every tag in use, sorted alphabetically, one per line. |\n| `tracker tags --count`               | `tag<TAB>count` rows, sorted by count desc then alpha. |\n| `tracker tags --json`                | `{ \"<tag>\": <count>, ... }` (for piping into jq). |\n| `tracker tags rename OLD NEW`        | Rewrite every item's `tags` list, replacing OLD with NEW. Idempotent (no-op if OLD absent or NEW already present). De-dupes when both already exist on the same item. |\n| `tracker tags merge OLD INTO_NEW`    | Alias for `rename` semantically; named distinctly so the audit trail reads sensibly when collapsing two parallel-evolved tags into one canonical name. |\n| `tracker tags describe TAG [TEXT]`   | Set / read a one-line purpose blurb for TAG. Persisted in a new `tag_descriptions` block in `.agent/tracker/config.yaml` (or a sibling `tag_catalog.yaml` — TBD in the impl PR). With no TEXT, prints the current description (or empty if unset). |\n| `tracker tags deprecate TAG [--in-favor-of NEW]` | Mark TAG as deprecated in the catalog. Surfaces in `tracker tags --count` output and in `tracker add --tag <deprecated>` warnings. Optional `--in-favor-of NEW` records the recommended replacement so future `tracker add --tag <deprecated>` invocations can suggest the canonical name. |\n\nThe lean-toward subcommand form (rather than a `list --list-tags` flag) is for discoverability — users running `--help` will see `tags` next to `show` / `list` / `ready` and recognize it. The rename / merge / describe / deprecate verbs make this a **catalog** rather than just an enumerator.\n\n**Scope of the minimum-viable cut** (so this doesn't grow into a multi-month item):\n- **Required:** `tags` (enum), `tags --count`, `tags --json`, `tags rename`, `tags describe`. These are the smallest set that close the playbook gap *and* let the user clean up duplicate tags in one pass.\n- **Stretch:** `tags merge` (could be implemented as a thin wrapper over `rename`), `tags deprecate` (needs catalog persistence + `tracker add` warning hook). File as child item if the main lift is bigger than expected.\n- **Out of scope:** tag hierarchies, tag aliases at filter time (`--tag dx` matching `developer_experience`), per-tier tag visibility. Add later if needed.\n\n**Implementation notes:**\n- Read-only enumeration is one pass over the compiled tier index (canonical ∪ workspace by default; add `--tier stealth` later if needed). Tags are already a field on every compiled item, so it's cheap.\n- `rename` writes are mutations: each affected item gets an op appended replacing its tag list. Auto-sync trigger fires once at the end (not per-item) — same as a batch `update` invocation. Locked items are skipped with a warning.\n- `describe` persistence: probably `tag_descriptions:` map in `.agent/tracker/config.yaml`, since that file already governs other cross-item config. Sibling-file `tag_catalog.yaml` is also fine — the implementation PR picks one and documents the choice.\n- Output stable / sorted so it's pipeable into `xargs` / `comm` / `diff` without surprises.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Empty corpus → empty output, exit 0.\n- Corpus with 3 items having tags `[a, b]` / `[b, c]` / `[a]` → `a\\nb\\nc\\n` (sorted) for `tags`; `[(\"a\",2),(\"b\",2),(\"c\",1)]` for `--count`.\n- `rename a → x` rewrites all `a` occurrences to `x`; idempotent on re-run; de-dupes when both exist.\n- `describe foo \"purpose text\"` round-trips via `describe foo` (no arg).\n- Tier filter respected (stealth-only items excluded by default).\n\n**Cross-references:**\n- WI-mobup-matih-folit-maraj-zigid-zopab-bibat-vokak — re-opened pending this; isbefore edge points there.\n- `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md` §\"When NOT to file a new tracker item\" point 1 (the call-site that this enables).\n- `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/cli.py` — where the new subcommand lands.\n- `.agent/tracker/config.yaml` — likely landing site for `describe` persistence."  # 879a
- op: discuss  # 2bba
  at: '2026-04-27T14:50:26Z'  # 2bba
  by: human  # 2bba
  actor: jgstern  # 2bba
  clock: 3  # 2bba
  nonce: 2bba  # 2bba
  message: "I think each tag should have a \"status\" attribute whose value is one of \"inactive\" or \"active\". A tag that is active means that it is affixed to at least 1 item; otherwise the tag is inactive. \n\nAlso, I think each tags should have a \"created_on\" attribute, a \"last_modified\" attribute, and a \"last_used\" attribute. created_on would be the timestamp of when the tag was created. last_modified would be when one of a few select attributes of the tag last changes, eg the name or the description or the status. last_used would be the timestamp of when the tag was last added to or removed from any item.\n\nWhat do you think?"  # 2bba
- op: discuss  # 5ee3
  at: '2026-04-27T14:55:44Z'  # 5ee3
  by: agent  # 5ee3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5ee3
  clock: 4  # 5ee3
  nonce: 5ee3  # 5ee3
  message: "I think those attributes are right. Three design questions worth resolving in this thread before the implementation PR commits to a shape:\n\n(1) **Is `status` stored or derived?** Two paths:\n\n  (a) **Stored.** `status` is a field on the catalog entry. `add`/`update` paths flip it on first use; `tags rename` flips it; `tags deprecate` could force-set it. Risk: divergence between stored status and reality if any code path mutates an item's tags without going through the catalog hooks.\n\n  (b) **Derived at read time.** `status` is computed from `count_of_items_with_this_tag`. `active` iff count > 0, `inactive` otherwise. No sync surface, no divergence risk. Cheap because `tags --count` already has to compute counts.\n\n  I lean (b) — derived. The user-visible behavior is the same (`tracker tags` shows status correctly), and the bug surface is strictly smaller. Storage cost matches: `tags --count` is the worst-case path either way.\n\n  This interacts with deprecation. The earlier description had a `tags deprecate` verb. If `status` is derived two-state, deprecation becomes a separate `deprecated: bool` field on the catalog entry — three external states (active / inactive / deprecated) computed from two underlying values (count, deprecated_flag). If you want `status` itself to literally be one of {active, inactive, deprecated}, we go (a) stored. Either is coherent; they differ on bug surface.\n\n(2) **created_on / last_used semantics — derived from the op log, or stored?**\n\n  Op-log derivation is appealing because `add`/`update` ops already carry timestamps and the data is reconstructible without new write paths — but it means every `tags` enumeration scans the full op log, which is slow on a long-history corpus (the tracker has hundreds of items × hundreds of ops apiece). Stored is the fast path: when a tag first appears in any item, we write a catalog entry with `created_on = now`; when a tag's count changes (item gains or loses it), we update `last_used = now`. That's two new write paths but each is one line in the existing `add`/`update` code.\n\n  I lean stored, with a one-time migration that walks the op log to backfill `created_on` and `last_used` for tags that already exist. Migration is run-once at first `tracker tags` invocation after deploy, or on `tracker init / setup` — cheap because it happens only once. After that, every operational mutation maintains the catalog incrementally.\n\n(3) **Where does the catalog live?** Two options that both already exist as sibling files:\n\n  (a) `.agent/tracker/config.yaml` — central tracker config. Adds a top-level `tag_descriptions:` (or similar) map. Risks: this file already governs other concerns (kind prefixes, sync threshold), so co-locating tag metadata grows the file's surface.\n\n  (b) `.agent/tracker/tag_catalog.yaml` — new sibling file dedicated to tag metadata. Cleaner separation, easier to inspect. Risks: one more file to keep in sync with the tier set.\n\n  Lean (b). The catalog is a first-class concept once it has timestamps and statuses; giving it its own file mirrors the way item ops live in their own `.ops` files rather than under `config.yaml`.\n\n**Proposed catalog schema** (folding in your four attributes):\n\n```yaml\ntags:\n  developer_experience:\n    description: 'Things that make agent / human developer life better.'\n    created_on: 2026-01-15T10:30:00Z\n    last_modified: 2026-04-20T14:00:00Z   # description, deprecated, or rename\n    last_used: 2026-04-27T14:50:00Z       # last add/remove on any item\n    deprecated: false                     # status derived from {count, deprecated}\n```\n\n`tracker tags` enumeration becomes:\n```\nTAG                        STATUS   COUNT  LAST USED              DEPRECATED\ndeveloper_experience       active   42     2026-04-27T14:50:00Z   no\nbakeoff_infra              inactive 0      2026-04-12T08:00:00Z   no\ndx                         inactive 0      —                      yes (in_favor_of=developer_experience)\n```\n\nIf you concur on (1)(b) derived, (2) stored with op-log backfill, (3)(b) sibling file — I'll update the WI-lifal description to lock those in and add a paragraph on the migration step. If you want different choices on any of (1)/(2)/(3), say which and I'll write them in instead.\n\nOne scope-creep guard: I'd hold off on per-tag visibility / hierarchies / filter-time aliases (out-of-scope list in the current description still applies). Catalog + 4 attributes + 5 verbs (enum / count / json / rename / describe / deprecate, with merge as wrapper over rename) is the coherent minimum-viable unit; anything beyond can ship later as child items."  # 5ee3
- op: discuss  # 6ea8
  at: '2026-04-27T15:03:51Z'  # 6ea8
  by: human  # 6ea8
  actor: jgstern  # 6ea8
  clock: 5  # 6ea8
  nonce: 6ea8  # 6ea8
  message: "I am leaning \"concur\" on  (1)(b) derived, (2) stored with op-log backfill, (3)(b) sibling file -- however, I am wondering about deprecated vs inactive vs active. Are we already using deprecated -- I feel like we might be? what is the distinction between deprecated and inactive?"  # 6ea8
- op: discuss  # 8e74
  at: '2026-04-27T15:05:57Z'  # 8e74
  by: agent  # 8e74
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8e74
  clock: 6  # 8e74
  nonce: '8e74'  # 8e74
  message: "Yes, 'deprecated' is already a tracker concept — `config.yaml` has `deprecated_statuses` per-kind (e.g. `work_item.deprecated_statuses: [holding]` and the AGENTS.md note 'Do NOT use `holding` — it is deprecated'). `validation.py` accepts deprecated values when reading historical ops but warns on new uses. So extending the same name to tags is **consistent with existing terminology** rather than introducing a new word.\n\n**The distinction I'm proposing — three states from two underlying values:**\n\n| Stored: `deprecated` flag | Computed: `count` | External `status` |\n|---|---|---|\n| false  | > 0 | **active**     |\n| false  | == 0 | **inactive**   |\n| true   | (any)| **deprecated** |\n\n`active` = current uses, no opinion needed.\n`inactive` = no current uses, no explicit retirement decision. Re-using is fine.\n`deprecated` = explicit retirement decision. `tracker add --tag <deprecated>` should warn (and ideally suggest the canonical replacement recorded in `in_favor_of`).\n\n**Concrete examples that show why both 'inactive' and 'deprecated' need to exist:**\n\n1. `bakeoff_infra` — tag drifted to `bakeoff_infrastructure` over time, last write was three months ago, count is now 0 but no one explicitly retired it. Status: **inactive**. If someone organically re-uses it, that's fine — maybe they want it back. No warning fires.\n\n2. `dx` — we explicitly run `tracker tags rename dx developer_experience` and then `tracker tags deprecate dx --in-favor-of developer_experience` to lock in the migration. Count is now 0 *and* the deprecated flag is set. Status: **deprecated**. Future `tracker add --tag dx` warns: 'tag dx is deprecated; use developer_experience instead'. Without the deprecated state, a future agent could re-introduce `dx` and we'd lose the migration we just paid for.\n\n3. Hybrid case: `old_tag` is deprecated *while* still affixed to 7 items (e.g. you mark deprecated as a *signal* and let cleanup happen organically). Count > 0, deprecated == true. Status: **deprecated**. Warning still fires on `tracker add --tag old_tag` to a new item, but existing usages aren't auto-rewritten. Cleanup is a separate `tracker tags rename` operation done on the human's schedule. (This mirrors how Python's `DeprecationWarning` works — the deprecated thing keeps working while you migrate.)\n\n**Why not collapse them?** If we made `status` literally one of {active, inactive, deprecated} (single stored field), we'd lose the orthogonality: a tag with 5 uses that's been deprecated couldn't be both 'currently used' AND 'should not be used in the future'. The two-axis model captures the real distinction (count is observable; deprecation is editorial).\n\nIf you concur on this:\n- I'll lock in (1)(b) derived status, (2) stored timestamps with op-log backfill, (3)(b) sibling `.agent/tracker/tag_catalog.yaml`, AND the three-state model with `deprecated: bool` + computed `status`.\n- The `tags deprecate` verb takes optional `--in-favor-of NEW` to record the canonical replacement.\n- `tracker add --tag <deprecated>` emits a non-blocking warning suggesting `--in-favor-of` if set.\n\nIf you'd prefer the simpler two-state model (status ∈ {active, inactive}) without separate deprecation, that's also coherent — we'd lose the migration-lock-in feature but gain a smaller catalog schema. Your call."  # 8e74
- op: discuss  # c5cd
  at: '2026-04-27T15:11:27Z'  # c5cd
  by: human  # c5cd
